# The project video, part two of two: a page of full-frame graphics.
#
#   ./build/fbtest --sequence /tmp/fb-b --script tools/video-tiles.cues \
#                  --file docs/example-tiles.png --columns 4 --rows 4 \
#                  --size 1920x1080 --seconds 20 --fps 30
#
# See tools/video.cues for part one, the syntax, and why there are two sheets.
#
# This part exists to say the thing part one cannot: a sheet does not have to be
# a sprite. Sixteen hard-edged monochrome cells, played first as full-frame
# graphics — which is Fit: Stretch and Key: None, a combination that would be
# wrong for every frame of part one — and then broken into a field.

# --------------------------------------------------- 0s: one cell, whole frame
# Fill covers the raster preserving the cell's aspect and crops the overflow,
# and None ignores the file's alpha, because a page like this has none worth
# honouring. Straight in at full frame: after twenty-four seconds of sprites on
# black, the cut to edge-to-edge op art is the point being made.
#
# Fill and not Stretch, though Stretch is the mode this sheet nominally
# demonstrates. Stretch maps the square cell onto 16:9 exactly, which turns the
# tile's circular window into an ellipse — correct, and it reads on screen as a
# squashed circle rather than as a deliberate choice. Fill crops the disc off
# the top and bottom instead, so what fills the frame is pure stripe with no
# edge in it at all.
0.0   Background Opacity=1.0
0.0   Fit=1
0.0   Key=3
0.0   Copies=1
0.0   Scale=0.57
0.0   Position X=0.5
0.0   Position Y=0.5
0.0   Rate=0.58
0.0   Mode=0
0.0   Frame Count=0
0.0   Stagger=0.0

# Ping-pong at 4s: the bars narrow through the run and widen back out, so the
# whole sheet breathes rather than snapping back to the first cell.
4.0   Mode=1

# ------------------------------------------------------------ 7s: into a field
# Back to the cell's own proportions and out into a grid. Sampling stays Smooth
# on purpose: these are the finest lines in either sheet, and Pixel here would
# alias into a mess — which is a real answer about when to use which, not a
# setting to hide.
7.0         Fit=0
7.0..9.5    Copies=1.0..12.0
7.0..9.5    Scale=0.57..0.30
7.0..9.5    Spread=0.6..0.58
8.0..11.0   Stagger=0.0..0.20

# ------------------------------------------------------------- 11s: the chase
# On a ring, where the travel round the circle is unmistakable, then scattered.
11.0        Arrange=1
11.0..13.0  Copies=12.0..14.0
11.0..13.0  Spread=0.58..0.46
11.0..13.0  Scale=0.30..0.32

14.5        Arrange=2
14.5..17.0  Copies=14.0..24.0
14.5..17.0  Spread=0.46..0.66
14.5..17.0  Scale=0.32..0.24
14.5..17.0  Stagger=0.20..0.34

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 17s: close
# Tint into a cold blue over the last three seconds. The sheet is pure black and
# white, so this is unambiguously the plugin and not the artwork — which is the
# right last thing to say about a plugin that plays somebody else's artwork.
17.0..20.0  Tint=1.0..0.35
17.0..20.0  Tint_Green=1.0..0.70
17.0..20.0  Spread=0.66..0.76
